typically a classroom  where we're spending 350 more today  i mean i don't know the numbers in  canada but i looked up in the uk and  it's largely similar  like there's more and more money going  into what is still essentially the same  format and i have grandchildren in  school here and it's the same format  the teacher decides on the curriculum in  fact what we have is  what i call the classroom triangle the  teacher has to teach a curriculum  there are tests to make sure that  whatever the curriculum was whatever was  taught  at the kids that the kids were able to  reproduce it  and the other thing the teacher worries  about is how to keep people busy in the  classroom  so basically the three preoccupations  are how do we teach the curriculum  how do i test if they learn anything and  how do i keep them occupied  in other words the teachers in charge  and  my belief is and this is another book  that i recommend  that's not how the brain learns in fact  the brain learns all the time  the brain is built to learn it'll always  learn it'll learn through  exposure it'll form patterns and it's in  its own in the brain will form patterns  in order that it can deal with these  situations that keep coming out  but the brain decides what it's going to  learn when and  the brain doesn't necessarily comply  with the curriculum that the teacher  or the the order that the teacher has  has decided  and the major issue is motivation  enjoyment  and alertness and so again it's it's a  matter of  training oneself to be attentive to  what's happening but the three are  related if you're not motivated you're  not enjoying it if you're not motivated  and enjoying it you aren't paying  attention so  those are the things that that  teachers have to worry about and  language learning or learning means  changing and there is also  some degree of resistance to learning  because when you learn you change and a  lot of people don't want to change  so if i bring that to the subject of  language learning which interests me  what i have found to be largely true is  what this gentleman stephen krashman  says  and that is to learn a language which is  my interest  all he says all you need is input  that's all you need if you have enough  input  that is reading and listening if you  have enough input  the brain which is used to learning and  likes to learn  the brain will start to form its own  patterns and labels to deal with the  language  and he says you know all the structures  we need to learn are  already there if you provide them with  enough comprehensible input  and of course you can't give people  incomprehensible input  you have to give people input that's of  interest to them  and comprehensible and  therefore we don't eat grammar says he i  don't fully agree  because i do believe that that there are  things you can do  to help the learner be more attentive to  what's happening in the language  because there are certain things you're  going to hear or read  and you aren't going to notice time and  time and time again  so there are things you can do to make  the brain more attentive  but the bigger thing is in my opinion  that we need to  i i can only speak from my experience as  a language learner  we need to kind of explode the  boundaries  and so that we don't deal with the  classroom situation with a teacher  kids in the class primary concern is to  push through the curriculum  get people to you know behave in the  classroom and past tests but that we  have learning communities  more like what  matt ridley referred to where people are  learning from each other  exchanging information languages  experiences and so forth and so on and i  think that is the promise  of the internet the promise that the  ipad makes  more accessible than ever before  and so if we get back to the real  learning  triangle is not curriculum tests  and people keeping people busy in the  classroom the real learning triangle is  attitude  time spent and attentiveness in 